argon-utils (0.1.25) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The UPS clock is looked at again after 10 minutes, not 6 hours, while
    the system clock is not NTP-synchronised. The first check runs seconds
    into a boot, before NTP has settled, and nothing may be corrected
    until it has -- so the UPS stayed uncorrected for hours (seen on the
    boot after T18's wake).

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:45:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.24) unstable; urgency=medium

  * argond names the config sections it does not find: an upgrade keeps
    the administrator's file, so a config written before a feature
    existed ran that feature's defaults with nothing said. A config with
    no [oled] left the display off and the tray's new switch hidden,
    with no visible cause.
  * argond also says when the case display is simply not enabled.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 10:45:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.23) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The tray gets a "Case display" checkbox that switches the OLED status
    page off and on, through argond (D-Bus OledState/SetOled, polkit
    action org.argonutils.oled: the active session may, without a
    password). Switched off, the page stays dark across restarts and
    reboots until switched on again. Offered only while argond is
    driving a display.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 22:30:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.22) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Binaries no longer carry the builder's home directory. Rust embeds
    source paths for panic messages, and every dependency's lay under
    ~/.cargo/registry, so each binary held ~120 "/home/<user>/..." strings.
    debian/rules remaps them (--remap-path-prefix), and the build now
    fails if any shipped binary still contains a build path.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 21:45:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * argon-tray uses the full-colour battery icons when an installed theme
    has them (AdwaitaLegacy, which the Raspberry Pi's PiXtrix inherits):
    the symbolic ones are drawn in one dark colour the Pi panel does not
    recolour, and all but vanished on a dark panel. --icons colour|symbolic
    overrides the choice; --once says which set is in use.

